    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d695">

                <pb facs="0774" n="662" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg662"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 18253/17.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum to the Italian Embassy.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1910-03-01"><hi rendition="#italic">March 1,
                        1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The department has examined very carefully the memorandum submitted by the
                    Italian Embassy on the 19th instant, relative to the case of Maiorano <hi
                        rendition="#italic">v</hi>. The Baltimore &amp; Ohio Railroad Co., but is
                    unable to reach a conclusion different from that heretofore expressed in the
                    matter, the Supreme Court of the United States having decided the question on
                    the broad ground that by a fair interpretation, the provisions of the treaty of
                    1871 did not confer upon nonresident aliens the same rights as are enjoyed by
                    actual residents in Pennsylvania, and that, therefore, in refusing to allow the
                    plaintiff, who had never been within the State of Pennsylvania, to recover
                    damages under a statute of that State, no treaty rights were violated.</p>
                <p>As stated in the concluding paragraph of the Supreme Court’s opinion— <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>If an Italian subject, sojourning in this country, is himself given all
                            the direct protection and security afforded by the laws to our own
                            people, including all rights of actions for himself or his personal
                            representatives to safeguard the protection and security, the treaty is
                            fully complied with without going further and giving to his nonresident
                            alien relative a right of action for damages for his death, although
                            such action is afforded to native resident relatives, and although the
                            existence of such an action may indirectly promote his safety.</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
